  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-cleaning-recoding">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-crop-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          TODO: change this now that the STACK q has changed.
          In your own words, explain why filtering the crop data to
          <c>sold == "yes"</c> before averaging ( ) produced a wrong answer even
          though the averaging itself was done correctly.
          Then describe one other variable, in any dataset you have worked with,
          where <q>missing</q> and <q>zero</q> would mean genuinely different
          things, and explain what filtering it out would silently assume.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-ebola-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-ebola-two-systems"/> describes case data being entered
          independently in two places, laboratory records and case investigation
          forms, with no process forcing the two back together.
          Suggest one concrete check, applied at the point of data entry rather
          than afterwards, that could have caught a duplicate case before it
          reached a national situation report.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-phe-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-phe-cost"/> notes that the PHE failure was dangerous
          specifically because it was silent: the file that lost data looked
          exactly like a file that hadn't.
          Suggest one concrete check that, if run automatically every time a
          file was produced, would have caught this problem within a day rather
          than a week.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-lake-labels">
      <statement>
        <p>
          TODO: amend this Using the <c>raw_lake</c> vector from
          "ss-clean-inconsistent-labels" as a starting point, write R code that
          produces a clean factor with exactly three levels, without using
          <c>tools::toTitleCase()</c>.
          There is more than one correct approach.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-duplicate-ids">
      <statement>
        <p>
          TODO: amend this Using the <c>bird_id</c> vector from
          "ss-clean-duplicates", write R code that returns the actual duplicated
          <em>values</em> (not just <c>TRUE</c> / <c>FALSE</c> flags).
          Then explain, in your own words, why a low count from
          <c>sum(duplicated(x))</c> is reassuring but not sufficient on its own
          <mdash/> what would you still need to check before trusting that the
          data has no duplicate-record problem?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-implausible-check">
      <statement>
        <p>
          TODO: amend this "ss-clean-implausible" catches an implausible bill
          length using a hard-coded range, <c>bill &lt; 50 | bill &gt; 200</c>.
          Suggest a plausible range check for <c>leg_length_mm</c> and for
          <c>body_mass_g</c>, using whatever you can infer from the numbers
          already given elsewhere in this module.
          What is the risk of setting a range check too narrow, as opposed to
          too wide?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-recode-mass-class">
      <statement>
        <p>
          TODO: amend this Recode <c>body_mass_g</c> into a new categorical
          variable <c>size_class</c> with two levels, <q>light</q> and
          <q>heavy</q>, split at 2500 g.
          Then cross-tabulate <c>size_class</c> against <c>species</c>.
          Does the split you chose separate the two species cleanly, or does it
          cut through one of them?
          What does that tell you about choosing a threshold for a recode versus
          choosing one for a plot?
        </p>
